\date{2025-11-29T03:31:29Z}
\title{Natural numbers}
\tag{discrete}
\tag{draft}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{We write #{\N} (or \tt{N}, or \tt{Nat}, or \tt{Natural}) for the [type](002B) of \em{natural numbers}.  Natural numbers include all of the non-negative counting numbers #{0, 1, 2, 3, \dots} and so on.}

\p{Note that mathematicians disagree on whether #{0} counts as a "natural number".  However, among computer scientists, having #{0} count as a natural number is fairly universal.  The important point, which we will [explore in more depth later](002E), is that the collection of non-negative counting numbers ##{0, 1, 2, 3, \dots} has much better mathematical properties than the collection ##{1, 2, 3, \dots} without zero, so it is much more elegant to take the former collection as "fundamental" and define the latter in terms of it.  But what we \em{call} these collections doesn't matter in any ultimate sense.}

\p{In any case, Disco agrees that zero is a natural number, as we can check:
\pre\verb<<<|
Disco> :type 0
0 : ℕ<<<
}